.\" Template for archivectl.1: scripts/gen-docs.py fills in the marked parts
.\" from the dispatcher, libexec/archivectl/*.sh and lib/*.sh, reading them only.
.TH ARCHIVECTL 1 2026-11-14 "archivectl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
archivectl \- encrypted, compressed BorgBackup archives of a directory tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY archivectl
[\fB\-h\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR\~.\|.\|.]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B archivectl
keeps one Borg repository per backed-up directory.
.B initialise
marks a directory as a backup source by writing a
.I .borgbackup
file into it, see
.BR archivectl.borgbackup (5),
and creates the repository under
.BR $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT ,
named after the directory's absolute path with every
.B %
written
.BR %25 ,
every
.B /
.B %2F
and every newline
.BR %0A ,
so that no two directories share a name, and encrypted with
.BR repokey .
A name longer than 199 bytes is cut to its first 135 bytes, followed by
.B ~
and the SHA\-256 of the path, 200 bytes in all, which no shorter name can
equal; the log names below are made the same way.
dedupctl named it with every
.B /
turned into
.B !
and every
.B !
doubled, which two directories can share: when a repository under that
name exists,
.B initialise
refuses and says so.
A repository that exists under the new name is used again.
.PP
Every other command looks for the nearest
.I .borgbackup
in the current directory or its parents, changes into that directory,
and reads the file.
An archive is named after the time it was started,
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD_HH:MM:SS ,
and the name is its id.
.PP
Selecting and acting are separate commands:
.B list
prints records, one per line, its fields separated by tabs and the name
first, and
.B delete
acts on the records it reads from standard input.
Standard output carries records only: those of
.B list
and
.BR diff ,
and the record of the archive
.B create
made.
An empty answer prints nothing.
In a path of a record, a backslash, tab or newline is written
.BR \e\e ,
.B \et
or
.BR \en .
Messages, progress, summaries and the output of
.B borg
go to standard error.
A name given on the command line must name an archive, or the command
ends with status 1 before it runs borg.
Every command that writes,
.BR initialise ,
.BR create ,
.B delete
and
.BR restore ,
takes
.BR \-n ,
which reports on standard error what it would do and writes nothing.
.PP
Arguments are read only after the command; the dispatcher itself knows
only
.BR \-h " and " \-\-help .
Every command refuses an option it does not know with status 2 before it
does anything, and every command takes
.BR \-h " and " \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show this help message and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "initialise"
.TP
\fBarchivectl initialise\fR [\fB\-n\fR] [<path>]
.PP
Make <path>, or the current directory, a backup source. It writes the .borgbackup of the directory and initialises its Borg repository, encrypted with repokey. init is an alias. Options:
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would be written and write nothing.
.SS "create"
.TP
\fBarchivectl create\fR [\fB\-n\fR]
.PP
Archive the source into a new archive and print its record. The archive is compressed with lz4. A parent directory's repository, if there is one, is then archived with the same options. Options:
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would be archived and write nothing, here or in a parent repository.
.PP
The archive is written with
.B borg create \-\-compression lz4 \-\-stats \-\-list \-\-filter=CE
from
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
leaving out the
.B EXCLUDES
of
.IR .borgbackup .
An archive of the same name is refused before borg runs.
borg runs with
.BR BORG_EXIT_CODES=modern ,
and its warnings, status 1 or 100 to 127, alone or together, keep the
archive: a file borg could not read is left out of it, and one that
changed while borg read it is stored as read.
borg names each such file on standard error, with
.B E
or
.B C
in front, and
.B create
writes those names to the log, prints the record and ends with status 0.
borg writes its output with
.BR \-\-log\-json ,
shown on standard error as text, so that these file names are never
taken from its messages.
The status is borg's own, not that of a command around it.
On a borg error the archive is deleted only if this run's borg committed
it, which the fingerprint it printed shows, and
.B create
ends with borg's status, after the parent below; a
.B SOURCE_DIR
that is no directory ends it with status 1, also after the parent.
.B \-n
runs
.B borg create \-\-dry\-run \-\-list
with its list on standard error and writes no log.
.PP
When a directory above the repository root has a
.I .borgbackup
of its own, an archive is then created there too, with the same options:
a
.B \-n
stays a dry run in the parent.
The parent's record goes to standard error, so that standard output
holds the record of this repository's archive only.
The parent's archive is created whatever became of this one; the status
is this archive's, or the parent's when this one was made.
.SS "list"
.TP
\fBarchivectl list\fR [\fB\-\-older\fR N] [\fB\-\-newer\fR N] [\fB\-\-first\fR N] [\fB\-\-last\fR N]
.PP
Print one record per archive, oldest first: name, date, original size in bytes. Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-older\fR N
Only the archives older than N days.
.TP
\fB\-\-newer\fR N
Only the archives newer than N days.
.TP
\fB\-\-first\fR N
Then only the oldest N of those.
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR N
Then only the newest N of those.
.PP
The date is the archive's start time as borg records it,
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM:SS ,
and the size its original size from
.BR "borg info" .
The options combine: the age options select first, then
.B \-\-first
and
.B \-\-last
take from what is left, in that order.
.SS "delete"
.TP
\fBarchivectl delete\fR [\fB\-n\fR]
.PP
Delete the archives whose names begin the lines read on standard input. It reads the records list prints, and does nothing without input. Options:
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would be deleted and delete nothing.
.PP
The first field of each line read is the name; the rest of the line is
ignored, and blank lines are skipped.
Every name must name an archive, or nothing is deleted and the status
is 1.
The archives are removed with one
.BR "borg delete" ;
the space they held is freed by
.BR "borg compact" .
.SS "restore"
.TP
\fBarchivectl restore\fR [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-\-to\fR <dir> | \fB\-\-mount\fR <dir>] <name> [<path>...]
.PP
Extract archive <name>, or only the given paths in it, into the source directory. Options:
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would be extracted or mounted and write nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR <dir>
Extract into <dir> instead.
.TP
\fB\-\-mount\fR <dir>
Mount the archive at <dir> instead.
.PP
The archive is extracted with
.B borg extract
into
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
the repository root with the configuration
.B initialise
writes, or into the directory of
.BR \-\-to ,
created when missing.
A
.I path
is a path in the archive, relative to the source, and everything below
it is extracted.
.B \-\-mount
mounts the archive, or the paths in it, with
.BR "borg mount" ,
which needs FUSE; unmount it with
.B borg umount
.IR dir .
.B \-n
runs
.B borg extract \-\-dry\-run \-\-list
with its list on standard error, and with
.B \-\-mount
only checks that the archive exists.
.SS "diff"
.TP
\fBarchivectl diff\fR [\fB\-\-scratch\fR <dir>] <name> [<name2>]
.PP
Print one record per path whose content or type differs from archive <name> to <name2>. Without <name2> the live source is compared: path, then added, removed or modified. Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-scratch\fR <dir>
Extract archive <name> under <dir> to compare it with the live source; that comparison needs it.
.PP
.B added
is a path only the second holds,
.B removed
a path only the first holds, and
.B modified
a path whose content or type differs, also one that is a file in one
and a directory or a link in the other.
Two archives are compared with
.BR "borg diff \-\-json\-lines" ,
which compares the chunks borg stored and reads no file data; changes of
times, owners and permissions alone are left out.
borg shows a byte that is not UTF\-8 as
.BR ? ;
a path holding
.B ?
is matched to its bytes in the listings of the two archives, which
.B borg list
writes with
.B PYTHONIOENCODING=unicode_escape
without loss: a path in the second archive only is added, one in the first
only removed, and one in both whose type, link target or content
.RB ( "borg list {sha256}" )
differs is modified.
When borg's records and the paths found that way are not as many, the
comparison is refused with status 1; one with
.B \-\-scratch
still works.
The live source is compared by extracting the archive into a directory
made under the one
.B \-\-scratch
names, which needs room for the whole archive and is removed afterwards,
and comparing the trees by content with
.B rsync \-\-dry\-run \-\-checksum \-\-delete
leaving out the
.BR EXCLUDES .
Without
.BR \-\-scratch ,
that comparison is refused with status 2, and so is a
.B \-\-scratch
inside the source, whose extraction would count as added.
.SS "check"
.TP
\fBarchivectl check\fR
.PP
Check the integrity of the repository with borg check.
.SS "init"
.PP
Same as \(oqarchivectl initialise\(cq; see initialise.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, also
.BR \-h " / " \-\-help
after any command or none,
.B initialise
on a directory that has a
.I .borgbackup
already, and
.B delete
with no input.
.TP
.B 1
No command or an unknown command (the usage text goes to standard
error), no
.I .borgbackup
was found in the current directory or its parents, a name names no
archive,
.B create
found an archive of its name or no
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
.B diff
of two archives could not tell paths borg shows alike apart,
.B initialise
found a repository under the old name, or another error.
A failing
.B borg
command, a
.B borg list
that checks a name among them, ends the run with its own status; in
.BR create ,
a warning does not.
.TP
.B 2
An option the command does not know or without its value,
.B initialise
given more than one path or a path that is not a directory,
.B restore
given no name or both
.B \-\-to
and
.BR \-\-mount ,
or
.B diff
given no name, more than two, or one without
.B \-\-scratch
or with one inside the source.
.TP
.B 3
.I .borgbackup
does not set a variable the command needs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BORG_BACKUP_ROOT
Where
.B initialise
creates repositories; default
.IR ~/.borg_backups .
.TP
.BR BORG_PASSPHRASE ", " BORG_PASSCOMMAND ", ..."
Read by
.B borg
itself, which every command runs; see
.BR borg (1).
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the logs are kept; default
.IR ~/.local/state .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .borgbackup
The backup source's configuration, in the directory it backs up; see
.BR archivectl.borgbackup (5).
.TP
.I $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT/<name>/
The repository
.B initialise
creates.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/archivectl/<name>.log
The log of a repository,
.I <name>
being its root's path turned into a file name as for the repository.
Every command that writes appends a line with the time per thing it
did.
The output of borg stays out of it, since borg can show the passphrase
there, and the log and its directory are readable by the user only.
A dry run writes no log.
Logs of earlier versions, one per archive in
.IR $BORG_REPO/logs/ ,
are left as they are.
.SH EXAMPLES
Delete the archives older than 30 days, after looking at what goes:
.PP
.RS
.EX
archivectl list \-\-older 30 | archivectl delete \-n
archivectl list \-\-older 30 | archivectl delete
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract notes.txt from the newest archive into /tmp/old:
.PP
.RS
.EX
archivectl restore \-\-to /tmp/old "$(archivectl list \-\-last 1 | cut \-f1)" notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR archivectl.borgbackup (5),
.BR borg (1),
.BR snapshotctl (1)
